# Force LF on checkout for everyone, regardless of the cloning machine's own
# core.autocrlf setting. Without this, a checkout on a machine with
# core.autocrlf=true (Git for Windows' own installer default) turns every
# "#!/usr/bin/env bash" into a CRLF-terminated shebang, which the kernel reads
# as looking for a binary literally named "bash\r" -- and every script in the
# repo fails to run. Confirmed reproducible: a plain `git clone` on a machine
# with that setting breaks scripts/extract_assets.sh this way.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit rather than relying on text=auto's content-sniffing to skip these.
*.png binary
*.vpk binary
*.bin binary
*.ttf binary
